{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792178311,"nonce":"qoA7VS8uEQ3MH6z5eZcqxu5Eiy4=","title":"canvaskit: subdivision cage modelling for smooth procedural shapes","message":"**Why**\n\n`Geometry` builds primitives only (box, sphere, cylinder, torus, plane, height field). A realistic procedural shape such as a car cannot be made from them: the README banner's car had to hand-roll lofts in the scene, fake wheel arches with flares laid over the tyres, and lay pillars over the glass as separate strips, because a loft is a tube with no openings and a mesh has one material.\n\n**What**\n\n- A control cage of polygons that Catmull-Clark subdivision smooths into a surface, with semi-sharp creases (DeRose et al. 1998) so character lines, window edges and arch lips stay crisp while panels flow.\n- Mirroring across x = 0 with the seam welded, so half a symmetric shape is modelled.\n- A material slot per face, built into one mesh per slot, so glass, paint and trim are regions of one surface.\n- Normals split across hard creases and between slots, smooth elsewhere.\n- A spline through points (centripetal Catmull-Rom) with positions and tangents, for profiles and character lines.\n- Closest-point queries on the subdivided surface, for placing details such as light strips and handles on it.\n\n**Tests**\n\nIn tests/Components.hx: counts after one subdivision of a cube, corners held by infinitely sharp creases, a semi-sharp crease between the two, mirror welding, outward winding, split normals, spline interpolation and tangents, closest points lying on the surface.","files":null}]}